    "q-159404": {
      "dom_id": "step-q159404",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.65$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=1.4$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=1.3,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$",
      "worked_solution": "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.65$ | $0.15$ | $0.8$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.8+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =2.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =2.6-{1.4}^{2} \\\\  & =0.64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.7+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =1.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =1.9-{1.3}^{2} \\\\  & =0.21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1)(1)f(1,1)+(3)(1)f(3,1)+(1)(2)f(1,2)+(3)(2)f(3,2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.65+3\\cdot 0.05+2\\cdot 0.15+6\\cdot 0.15 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =2-1.4\\cdot 1.3 \\\\  & =0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.64 & 0.18 \\\\ 0.18 & 0.21\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-159404-a",
