    "q-159405": {
      "dom_id": "step-q159405",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}y({x}^{2}+1),\\, & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=0$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{2}{3},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$",
      "worked_solution": "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}(\\frac{3}{4}y({x}^{2}+1))\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{4}+{x}^{2})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{16}{15}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}-{(0)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}(\\frac{3}{4}y({x}^{2}+1))\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x]}_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$ The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle\n\n$$\nS=[-1,1]\\times [0,1].\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=g(x)\\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)=\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+1)$ and $h(y)=2y.$\n\nHence, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{18}\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
