    "q-148186": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148186",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}xy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{4}{3}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{8}{3},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$",
      "worked_solution": "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{16}xy)\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{4}y{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =2-{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\frac{1}{16}xy)\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{y}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{y}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =8-{(\\frac{8}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$ The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle\n\n$$\nS=[0,2]\\times [0,4].\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=g(x)\\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $h(y)=\\frac{y}{8}.$\n\nHence, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
